    "question": {
      ":question/id": "230f43bd-cdad-584f-a732-c50e27553660",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${12}^{\u2218}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the chocolate? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Write the equation in terms of the given constant $k$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={12}^{\u2218}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-12).\n$$",
